# Documented contract for .env (gitignored, rendered by scripts/render.py
# from secrets/symphony.sops.yaml). This file is the reference for what
# keys exist -- copy it to .env and fill in values by hand only if you're
# not using the render script.

TZ=America/Los_Angeles

# InfluxDB first-boot init (only takes effect against an empty
# influxdb-config volume; DOCKER_INFLUXDB_INIT_MODE=setup is a no-op once
# the volume already has an org/bucket).
DOCKER_INFLUXDB_INIT_MODE=setup
DOCKER_INFLUXDB_INIT_USERNAME=
DOCKER_INFLUXDB_INIT_PASSWORD=
DOCKER_INFLUXDB_INIT_ORG=
DOCKER_INFLUXDB_INIT_BUCKET=signalk
DOCKER_INFLUXDB_INIT_RETENTION=30d

# Read by grafana/provisioning/datasources/influxdb.yaml via Grafana's own
# `$INFLUX_TOKEN` provisioning-file expansion. Needs env_file: .env on the
# grafana service (see compose-grafana.yml) to actually reach the container.
INFLUX_TOKEN=

# The rest of Grafana's datasource, expanded the same way. INFLUX_URL is the
# one that changes during the move off systemd: use http://influxdb:8086 when
# InfluxDB is a compose service on symphony-net, or
# http://host.docker.internal:8086 while Grafana is containerized and InfluxDB
# is still native (compose-grafana.yml maps that name to the host gateway).
# INFLUX_ORG is the org the running database was initialised with, which on
# the boat is `symphony`; a datasource naming a different org authenticates
# and then silently returns no rows.
INFLUX_URL=http://influxdb:8086
INFLUX_ORG=
# Buckets, named after whoever writes them. Retention is a per-bucket
# property, which is the point of the split: one bucket cannot express "years
# of state-of-charge, two weeks of CPU".
#
#   signalk          vessel data, full rate
#   signalk_archive  the same paths decimated to 1/min, long retention
#   telegraf         host metrics
#   influxdb         InfluxDB's own scraped metrics
#
# The bucket is named in each Flux query, not in the datasource, so
# INFLUX_BUCKET is only Grafana's default for ad-hoc exploration -- the
# provisioned dashboards reach all four through one datasource and one token.
INFLUX_BUCKET=signalk

# Telegraf's write path into InfluxDB, read via an EnvironmentFile drop-in on
# telegraf.service. Separate from INFLUX_TOKEN so the stopgap credential can
# be swapped for a scoped one without disturbing Grafana's datasource.
TELEGRAF_INFLUX_TOKEN=
TELEGRAF_INFLUX_ORG=
TELEGRAF_INFLUX_BUCKET=telegraf

# Grafana's built-in superadmin account. Only takes effect on a fresh
# grafana-data volume; on an existing one, also run (grafana-cli was folded
# into the main `grafana` binary as of Grafana 10+; use whichever your
# image ships):
#   docker exec grafana grafana cli admin reset-admin-password '<value>'
# Regular Grafana users (e.g. captain) are provisioned separately by
# scripts/provision_grafana_users.sh -- see RUNBOOK.md.
GF_SECURITY_ADMIN_USER=
GF_SECURITY_ADMIN_PASSWORD=

# The boat's DNS domain (e.g. example.com). Hostnames signalk.<DOMAIN> and
# grafana.<DOMAIN> must have A records pointing at the boat's LAN IP, in
# Cloudflare DNS. On a local dev machine the placeholder value is fine --
# Google login and the caddy TLS proxy only function on the real instance.
DOMAIN=
# Let's Encrypt registration/expiry-notice address.
ACME_EMAIL=
# Cloudflare API token scoped to Zone / DNS / Edit on DOMAIN's zone only,
# used by caddy for DNS-01 certificate issuance.
CLOUDFLARE_API_TOKEN=

# SignalK SSO (native OIDC, signalk-server >= 2.30), pointed at the boat's
# own Dex issuer (auth.<DOMAIN>, see dex/config.yaml.j2), which hands the
# login to GitHub or Google. Every SSO login gets DEFAULT_PERMISSION
# (readonly); changing anything still takes the local captain login.
# Don't point ADMIN_GROUPS at email addresses -- the mapper only accepts
# arrays of IdP group names, and these providers send none.
# CLIENT_ID/SECRET are the "symphony" static client in dex/config.yaml.j2.
SIGNALK_OIDC_ENABLED=true
SIGNALK_OIDC_ISSUER=
SIGNALK_OIDC_CLIENT_ID=symphony
SIGNALK_OIDC_CLIENT_SECRET=
SIGNALK_OIDC_REDIRECT_URI=
SIGNALK_OIDC_SCOPE=openid email profile
SIGNALK_OIDC_DEFAULT_PERMISSION=readonly
SIGNALK_OIDC_PROVIDER_NAME=GitHub / Google
SIGNALK_OIDC_AUTO_LOGIN=false

# Grafana against the same Dex client. Roles key off the login's email:
# ROLE_ATTRIBUTE_PATH is a hand-managed list (owner -> Admin), and
# ROLE_ATTRIBUTE_STRICT=true refuses any login that maps to nothing. To
# give a crew member Editor, extend the path in .env.j2:
#   email=='<owner>' && 'Admin' || email=='<crew>' && 'Editor'
# USE_REFRESH_TOKEN stays false: when true, Grafana signs users out about
# an hour after the boat loses internet. The long session lifetimes are
# what keep dockside logins alive offshore.
GF_SERVER_ROOT_URL=
GF_AUTH_GENERIC_OAUTH_ENABLED=true
GF_AUTH_GENERIC_OAUTH_NAME=GitHub / Google
GF_AUTH_GENERIC_OAUTH_CLIENT_ID=symphony
GF_AUTH_GENERIC_OAUTH_CLIENT_SECRET=
GF_AUTH_GENERIC_OAUTH_SCOPES=openid email profile
GF_AUTH_GENERIC_OAUTH_AUTH_URL=
GF_AUTH_GENERIC_OAUTH_TOKEN_URL=
GF_AUTH_GENERIC_OAUTH_API_URL=
GF_AUTH_GENERIC_OAUTH_ALLOW_SIGN_UP=true
GF_AUTH_GENERIC_OAUTH_USE_REFRESH_TOKEN=false
GF_AUTH_GENERIC_OAUTH_ROLE_ATTRIBUTE_PATH=
GF_AUTH_GENERIC_OAUTH_ROLE_ATTRIBUTE_STRICT=true
GF_AUTH_LOGIN_MAXIMUM_INACTIVE_LIFETIME_DURATION=30d
GF_AUTH_LOGIN_MAXIMUM_LIFETIME_DURATION=90d
